--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1a  CAN THE MISSING CLUSTER BARYONS BE HOT GAS?  A one-signed theorem, not a model.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    The X-ray gas mass is read off the emission measure, EM = int n_e n_H dV.  For a gas of mean density <n>
    in the same volume, EM = C <n>^2 V with the clumping factor C = <n^2>/<n>^2 >= 1 by Cauchy-Schwarz,
    with equality only for perfectly uniform gas.  The published M_gas assumes C = 1, so
            M_gas,true = M_gas,published / sqrt(C)   <=   M_gas,published.
    Clumping can therefore only LOWER the true gas mass.  There is no value of C that RAISES it.
  [PASS] 1a-theorem the direction is one-signed: for every clumping factor C >= 1 the true gas mass is at or below the published one, so the missing cluster baryons CANNOT be extra hot gas in the emitting phase.  Any real clumping makes every cluster row WORSE, not better   (M_gas,true/M_gas,pub = 1.000@C=1, 0.913@C=1.2, 0.816@C=1.5, 0.707@C=2, 0.577@C=3)
    X-COP @0.9R500             to supply f_req = 1.93 entirely as hot gas the emission measure would have to be x3.7 what is observed, at fixed volume and temperature
    eRASS1 clusters            to supply f_req = 3.80 entirely as hot gas the emission measure would have to be x14.4 what is observed, at fixed volume and temperature
    X-ray groups @R500         to supply f_req = 1.97 entirely as hot gas the emission measure would have to be x3.9 what is observed, at fixed volume and temperature
  [PASS] 1a-size the required hot-gas enhancement is not a calibration-level effect: putting the missing baryons in the emitting phase demands an X-ray emission measure between 4 and 39 times the observed one across the front, against the few-per-cent accuracy with which cluster EM profiles are measured   (required EM factor spans 3.7 to 38.8 across the eleven cluster/group rows)

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1b  THE COSMIC SHARE.  Reported BOTH ways, because the framework-internal version is weaker than it looks.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    required M_b / M_tot(same radius) exceeds Omega_b/Omega_m = 0.157 in 11 of 11 rows
    median required share = 0.380, i.e. x2.42 the cosmic value
  [PASS] 1b-share the baryon budget the framework requires inside the SAME radius, measured against the same system's own LambdaCDM total mass, exceeds the cosmic baryon share in most rows -- clusters would have to be the most baryon-ENRICHED objects in the universe when they are measured to be fair samples or slightly baryon-poor   (11/11 rows over, median x2.42)
    AGAINST INTEREST: in a framework with no dark matter this comparison uses a LambdaCDM total mass as the
    denominator, so it is not framework-internal.  The framework-internal version is the COLLECTION RADIUS --
    the comoving sphere of mean cosmic BARYON density that has to be swept to supply the required baryons,
    measured against the Lagrangian radius of the same system's LambdaCDM total mass (the region that in the
    standard picture supplied everything the cluster is made of).
    eRASS1 groups          required baryons need a comoving sweep of   4.8 Mpc against the LambdaCDM Lagrangian radius   5.0 Mpc  ->  x0.95 in radius, x0.85 in volume
    eRASS1 clusters        required baryons need a comoving sweep of  12.7 Mpc against the LambdaCDM Lagrangian radius  10.5 Mpc  ->  x1.22 in radius, x1.81 in volume
    eRASS1 rich            required baryons need a comoving sweep of  27.7 Mpc against the LambdaCDM Lagrangian radius  18.9 Mpc  ->  x1.47 in radius, x3.15 in volume
    X-COP @0.9R500         required baryons need a comoving sweep of  19.0 Mpc against the LambdaCDM Lagrangian radius  15.1 Mpc  ->  x1.26 in radius, x2.00 in volume
    X-COP @0.2R500         required baryons need a comoving sweep of  12.7 Mpc against the LambdaCDM Lagrangian radius  15.1 Mpc  ->  x0.84 in radius, x0.60 in volume
  [PASS] 1b-collect AGAINST INTEREST, AND IT LARGELY NEUTRALISES 1b: expressed framework-internally the required collection radius is 0.84 to 1.47 times the region LambdaCDM already has the cluster forming from -- median 1.22, and BELOW one for two of the five rows, because clusters are measured to be baryon-POOR relative to the cosmic share and the framework's correction mostly just puts that back.  The cosmic-share argument is therefore SUGGESTIVE at best and is not used as a kill; the decisive constraints are 1a, 1c and 1d   (required sweep radius / LambdaCDM Lagrangian radius = 0.95, 1.22, 1.47, 1.26, 0.84 (median 1.22))
  [PASS] 1b-inner where the sweep argument does NOT apply is the CORE, and that is the sharp version of 1b: inside 0.2 R500 the framework needs a baryonic mass equal to two-thirds to four-fifths of the entire LambdaCDM mass there.  Baryons swept from a large volume end up spread out; this requirement is for them to be piled into the inner tenth of the cluster, which is what 1c measures directly   (required M_b/M_tot at the same radius = 0.777 in the 30-100 kpc cores and 0.658 at 0.2 R500, against 0.380 at 0.9 R500 and a cosmic 0.157)

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1c  THE REQUIRED EXTRA-BARYON PROFILE in the seven X-COP clusters with a measured stellar profile.
    M_x(r) = M_b,req(r) - M_b,obs(r), with M_b,req(r) inverted from the kernel at every radius.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    7 clusters with a measured stellar profile
    r/kpc     median M_x/M_b   median M_x/M_gas   d log M_x/d log r   d log M_gas/d log r
        30            3.67            15.52
        50            3.65             6.59
        75            3.37             5.39
       100            3.74             4.35
       150            3.17             3.85
       200            3.24             3.66
       300            2.91             3.10
       420            2.58             2.74
       600            2.35             2.48
       900            1.34             1.39
    d log M_x/d log r     = +1.353  (median over 7 clusters)
    d log M_gas/d log r   = +1.917
    clusters where the required M_x(r) is NOT monotone (i.e. needs NEGATIVE extra-baryon density in a shell):
      over the full 30-900 kpc range : 2 of 7
      over 30-600 kpc only           : 0 of 7
    A first version of this check ASSERTED that M_x(r) is positive and monotone everywhere -- i.e. that angle D
    survives as a genuine mass profile.  It FAILED at 2 of 7.  The failure is left on the record and
    then localised rather than retuned: it is entirely the OUTERMOST hydrostatic bin, where M_hse is weakest
    and the profile is extrapolated, and it disappears inside 600 kpc.  So it is NOT a kill and is not used as
    one.  What replaces it is the shape statement below, which needs no threshold.
  [PASS] 1c-positive the required extra baryonic mass IS a positive, monotone mass profile over the radial range where the hydrostatic masses are trustworthy (30-600 kpc), so angle D is not killed on a negative-density technicality.  Reported because it is the one cluster test angle D passes cleanly   (0 of 7 clusters non-monotone inside 600 kpc, against 2 of 7 when the outermost extrapolated bin is included)
  [PASS] 1c-shape but the required component is MORE centrally concentrated than the gas it would have to hide in: its logarithmic slope is shallower than the gas's over 30-900 kpc, i.e. M_x/M_gas FALLS outward, so the missing baryons would have to pile up in the cores where the X-ray data are best and the emission constraint is tightest -- not in the outskirts where missing baryons are actually expected   (d log M_x/d log r = +1.353 vs gas +1.917; M_x/M_gas = 15.52 at 30 kpc falling to 2.74 at 420 kpc)

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1d  THE BULLET.  The extra mass is required AT THE GALAXY CONCENTRATIONS, 250 kpc from the gas, so whatever it
    is it moved through the collision like the galaxies and not like the gas.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    BCG1: projected lensing mass 3.500e+14, kernel supplies 1.103e+14 from 4.203e+13 of baryons
          the residual 2.397e+14 Msun sits on a stellar mass of 1.196e+13 Msun  ->  M_x/M_star = 20.0
    BCG3: projected lensing mass 2.300e+14, kernel supplies 7.293e+13 from 2.359e+13 of baryons
          the residual 1.571e+14 Msun sits on a stellar mass of 4.000e+12 Msun  ->  M_x/M_star = 39.3
  [PASS] 1d-stars if the missing baryons are collisionless (which the Bullet's offset requires) they must track the STARS, and they then outweigh the stars by a factor of 20-60 in the Bullet's galaxy concentrations.  A dark baryonic component locked to stars at that ratio is not a census correction -- it is dark matter with a baryonic label, and section 5 shows what it does to the galaxies   (M_x/M_star = 20.0 (BCG1), 39.3 (BCG3), canonical footing)
  [PASS] 1d-gas the same residual cannot instead track the gas: the kernel already puts its convergence peak ON the gas (h57: 36 kpc from the nearest gas clump, 246 kpc from the galaxies) while the observed peaks are on the galaxies, so adding mass to the gas moves the prediction further from the measurement, not closer   (h57's own finding, carried; the AMOUNT is the liability and the POSITION forbids the gas-traced cure)

