    N rows = 34;  f_req spans 0.053 to 58.3, a factor 1094
    rows requiring MORE baryons (f_req > 1): 24
    rows requiring FEWER baryons (f_req < 1): 10  ->  Pal 14, Pal 3, LG field dwarfs, NGC1052-DF2, Pal 4, NGC1052-DF4, NGC 2419, ATLAS3D (Salpeter), Tidal dwarfs (EFE on), Milky Way K_z
  [PASS] 2-sign THE SIGN CENSUS, and it is the structural objection to angle D as posed.  A census DEFICIT is one-signed: you cannot find fewer baryons than are already imaged.  Yet a substantial minority of the ledger requires the baryons to be REMOVED, and those rows are not marginal -- NGC1052-DF4 needs 6% of its measured stars, Pal 4 needs 22%, the tidal dwarfs 41%.  The hypothesis 'the censuses are systematically low' therefore cannot address them at all, whatever it does for the clusters   (10 of 34 rows need f_req < 1; the most extreme is NGC1052-DF4 at f_req = 0.053)

    what the f_req < 1 rows do to the stellar mass-to-light ratio (SPS floor for old populations: 1.3-2.2 in V):
