      Milky Way K_z            f_req = 0.693  ->  Upsilon_V = 1.39 (carried at Upsilon_V = 2 in the source item)
  [PASS] 2-floor and the negative rows are not rescuable by lowering Upsilon either: three of the four outer-halo globulars are driven below the stellar-population floor, which is a measurement and not an assumption   (3 star-cluster rows need Upsilon_V < 1.3 (SPS floor); Pal 4 needs 0.22)

==========================================================================================================================
3.  THE DISCRIMINATING QUESTION: is the required excess a CONSTANT fraction, or does it scale with something?
==========================================================================================================================
    N = 24 rows with f_req >= 1 (the only ones a census DEFICIT can address)
    raw spread of log f_req                        = 0.403 dex   (f_req = 1.19 to 58.3)
    d log f_req/d log M_b    = -0.064   r = -0.446   residual scatter 0.369 dex
    d log f_req/d log y_bar  = -0.277   r = -0.773   residual scatter 0.261 dex
    d log f_req/d log r      = -0.051   r = -0.172   residual scatter 0.406 dex
  [PASS] 3-constant the required excess is NOT a constant fraction and it is NOT a clean function of any single variable in the table.  Over 7.4 decades of baryonic mass it spans a factor 60 with 0.3 dex of residual scatter about the best single-variable fit -- a missing baryonic component would have to know about mass, acceleration and object type at once   (raw spread 0.403 dex; best single-variable fit leaves 0.261 dex (mass 0.369, acceleration 0.261))

    RESTRICTED TO THE ELEVEN CLUSTER/GROUP ROWS, where a missing hot-baryon component is most plausible:
      raw spread of log f_req = 0.158 dex (f_req = 1.93 to 6.23)
      d log f_req/d log M_b   = -0.096  r = -0.490  residual 0.146 dex
      d log f_req/d log y_bar = -0.027  r = -0.099  residual 0.166 dex
  [PASS] 3-cluster inside the cluster front alone the required multiplier is much closer to one number (f_req = 2-6, 0.16 dex) than it is across the ledger, and it has no significant mass or acceleration trend.  This is the strongest single fact FOR angle D and it is reported first: if the clusters were the whole ledger, 'one missing baryonic component of about 3-4x the observed baryons' would be a fair description of them   (cluster-only spread 0.158 dex about a median f_req = 3.80)

    IS THE MISSING COMPONENT WHERE THE HOT GAS IS?  If the missing baryons are an undetected phase of the
    intracluster medium, then the systems with the largest measured hot-gas content should need the largest
    correction.  This is testable inside the cluster front alone.
      measured d log f_req/d log f_gas = -0.327  (r = -0.553, residual 0.139 dex)
      the slope a pure M_gas error would induce, from the shared variable = -0.833
      difference from the degeneracy direction = +0.506
  [PASS] 3-hotgas BUG PATTERN 5 CHECKED BEFORE THE NUMBER IS QUOTED.  f_req and f_gas share M_gas, so a baryon-budget error drives rows along a slope of -0.83 in this plane by construction.  The measured slope is -0.33, i.e. HALF the degeneracy direction and 0.51 away from it, so the plane does carry information -- and what it carries is negative for the hypothesis: the correction does not rise with the measured hot-gas content, it falls.  The systems with the least hot gas need the most missing hot gas   (measured -0.327 against the degeneracy direction -0.833 and against the POSITIVE slope a hot-phase deficit predicts; the two eRASS1 rungs make the point without any fit -- groups have the LOWEST measured hot-gas share (0.020) and need the LARGEST correction (6.23), rich clusters the highest share (0.110) and need 3.60)
  [PASS] 3-transfer but the cluster number does NOT transfer.  Applying the clusters' own f_req to the rest of the ledger misses by a median 0.5 dex, and it has the wrong sign for a third of the rows outright.  A missing component calibrated on clusters is a cluster fudge, not a unification   (median |log10(f_req/f_cluster)| = 0.462 dex over 13 non-cluster rows with f_req >= 1, plus 10 rows of the wrong sign entirely)

==========================================================================================================================
4.  THE CANDIDATE BARYON LAWS.  Each is fitted to the ledger rows it claims to explain, then handed to section 5.
==========================================================================================================================

  law                                   params   fitted value              rms residual (dex)
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  D1  f = A                              1       A = 3.55                  0.403
  D1c f = A, clusters only               1       A = 3.65                  0.158
  D2/D3 f = 1 + A f_gas                  1       A = 3.45                  0.571
  D2/D3 clusters only                    1       A = 3.33                  0.184
  D4  f = 1 + A f_star                   1       A = 3.78                  0.470
  D4  clusters only                      1       A = 13.18                  0.220
  D5  f = A (M_b/1e11)^p                 2       A = 3.50, p = -0.065     0.369
  D6  f = A (g_bar/a0)^q                 2       A = 1.85, q = -0.275     0.262
  D8  one A per class (the null)        24      by construction exact       0.000
  [PASS] 4-fit no one-parameter baryon law comes close to the ledger.  The best single-variable law leaves 0.2-0.4 dex of residual against a raw spread of 0.3, i.e. it explains a minority of the variance, and every one of them is fitted only to the rows of the right sign in the first place   (best rms over the whole positive ledger = 0.262 dex against a raw spread of 0.403 dex)

  D6 IS NOT A BARYON LAW AT ALL -- a theorem.  A correction f(y) applied to g_bar produces the prediction
  g = nu(f(y) y) f(y) g_bar, which is a function of g_bar alone, i.e. a DIFFERENT KERNEL nu'(y) = f(y) nu(f(y) y).
  With the fitted D6 the implied kernel is nu'(y) = 1.85 y^-0.275 nu(1.85 y^+0.725), which is monotone in g.
  [PASS] 4-D6theorem a baryon correction that depends on acceleration is a kernel change wearing a census costume: it is not an independent hypothesis and it inherits every constraint on the kernel.  Stated as a theorem and verified on the fitted form   (nu'(y) = f(y) nu(f(y) y) with f = 1.85 y^(-0.275); the implied nu' is monotone)

==========================================================================================================================
5.  THE DECIDING COLUMN: what each law does to the GALACTIC SUCCESSES it must not break.
==========================================================================================================================
    SPARC: 147 galaxies, 3140 points

5.0  TWO INVARIANCE THEOREMS, verified numerically, that decide what a census correction can do at all.

  T1  SHAPE INVARIANCE.  Under (M_b -> f M_b, a_0 -> f a_0) the prediction nu(g_bar/a_0) g_bar is multiplied by
      f EXACTLY at every acceleration.  So a uniform census error preserves the RAR SHAPE perfectly -- and
      leaves a RIGID -log10(f) displacement that no further choice of a_0 can remove.
  [PASS] 5-T1 shape invariance holds to machine precision on all 3140 SPARC points.  Its consequence is the one that matters: the a_0 that keeps the RAR's SHAPE right is f times the old one, and at that a_0 the whole relation is displaced vertically by log10(f) -- 0.56 dex for the clusters' own factor, against an RAR whose scatter is 0.126 dex   (worst |residual(f) - (residual(1) - log10 f)| = 5.0e-16 for f = 2, 3.65, 10; the irremovable displacement is log10(f) = 0.562 dex at f = 3.65)

  T2  DEEP-LIMIT INVARIANCE.  Under (M_b -> f M_b, a_0 -> a_0/f) the deep-MOND prediction sqrt(G M_b a_0)/r is
      EXACTLY unchanged.  Since a_0 = kappa c sqrt(G rho_Lambda) with kappa FITTED (kappa = 1/2 has never been
      derived in this programme), the deep-tail refit absorbs the whole of a uniform census error into kappa,
      and every deep-MOND liability comes back unchanged.
      The departure from exactness is the kernel's own sub-leading term and it is a FACTOR OF ORDER
      1 + (f-1) sqrt(y)/2 -- so this escape leaks precisely where the systems are NOT deep:
        y = 1e-02   departure +1.358e-01   predicted (f-1)sqrt(y)/2 = +1.325e-01
        y = 1e-04   departure +1.329e-02   predicted (f-1)sqrt(y)/2 = +1.325e-02
        y = 1e-06   departure +1.325e-03   predicted (f-1)sqrt(y)/2 = +1.325e-03
        y = 1e-08   departure +1.325e-04   predicted (f-1)sqrt(y)/2 = +1.325e-04
  [PASS] 5-T2 deep-limit invariance verified by its CONVERGENCE RATE rather than by a tolerance: the departure falls as sqrt(y) with exactly the predicted coefficient (f-1)/2 over four decades of acceleration.  A uniform census error is therefore a relabelling of the fitted constant kappa in the deep limit, and it buys real ground only at accelerations near a_0   (worst |measured/(f-1)sqrt(y)/2 - 1| = 0.025 over y = 1e-8 to 1e-2 at f = 3.65)

  WHAT T1 AND T2 TOGETHER SAY.  They require DIFFERENT a_0 -- f a_0 and a_0/f, a factor f^2 apart.  A uniform
  census correction therefore has to choose: keep the RAR's shape and wear a rigid log10(f) displacement of the
  whole relation (T1), or keep the deep-MOND normalisation and move the transition scale by f^2 (T2).  It
  cannot do both, and in neither branch does it close a deep liability.  What it CAN reach is the band around
  a_0 itself, by the (f-1)sqrt(y)/2 leak -- which is +8% at the eRASS1 groups' y = 0.004 and +95% at the X-COP
  cores' y = 0.52.  A uniform census error is a CORE-ONLY escape.
  CONSEQUENCE, and it is the single most useful result in this section: the ONLY baryon laws that can do
  anything at all are DIFFERENTIAL ones -- laws whose factor differs between the systems that fail and the
  systems that work.  Every law below is therefore tested with the framework's OWN a_0 estimator re-run after
  the law is applied, so that its uniform part is removed and only its differential part is judged.

    BASELINE (no baryon law):
