    eRASS1 clusters        1.415e+13    5.374e+13     x 2.80        1.750e+14      x12.37
    X-COP @0.9R500         9.192e+13    1.775e+14     x 0.93        4.011e+14      x 4.36
    X-ray groups @R500     3.792e+12    7.479e+12     x 0.97        3.571e+13      x 9.42
  [PASS] 6-alt the alternative computed beside, and it is the honest point in the framework's favour: on the SAME systems LambdaCDM's dark component is a median x9.4 the measured baryons, while the framework's missing baryons are a median x2.8.  The kernel removes most of the discrepancy.  What angle D then has to do is find the remainder in baryons, and that remainder is still larger than every baryonic reservoir that has ever been detected   (LambdaCDM dark/baryon = x9.4 (range 4.4-45.6); framework missing-baryon/baryon = x2.8 (range 0.9-5.2))

==========================================================================================================================
7.  VERDICT ON ANGLE D
==========================================================================================================================
  THE BEST CASE FIRST.  The cluster/group front is a genuinely good fit to angle D as posed: eleven rows over
     four decades of mass agree on a required baryon multiplier of 3.8 to within 0.16 dex, with no
     significant mass or acceleration trend inside the front.  'The cluster baryon census is short by a factor
     of about three or four' is a fair one-parameter description of eleven independent measurements, and the
     framework asks LESS of the census than LambdaCDM asks of the dark sector on the same systems
     (x2.8 the baryons against x9.4).

  WHAT KILLS IT, in order of strength.
  1. THE SIGN.  A census deficit is one-signed.  10 of 34 ledger rows require the
     baryons to be REMOVED, down to 5% of the measured stars in NGC1052-DF4 and 11% in Pal 4, and three of the
     four outer-halo globulars are driven below the stellar-population floor.  Angle D cannot address those
     rows at all, in principle, whatever it does for the clusters.
  2. THE INVARIANCE THEOREMS.  A UNIFORM census correction is not a free move and it is not a null move: T1
     and T2 require a_0 a factor f^2 apart, so it must either displace the whole RAR by log10(f) or move the
     transition scale by f^2.  With a_0 refitted the framework's own way it buys back only the (f-1)sqrt(y)/2
     leak -- +8% at the eRASS1 groups, +95% in the X-COP cores -- and pays kappa = 0.483 -> 0.05.  Only
     DIFFERENTIAL laws can do real work, and a differential law is by construction a statement that the census
     is wrong by different amounts in different systems.
  3. THE KEEPERS.  Every differential law that touches the galaxies breaks something measurable: the RAR
     acquires a gas-fraction-dependent residual six times the observed one, the inner-diversity and Renzo
     correlations fall, and BOTH derived keepers -- the halo surface-density constant a_0/(2 pi G), which the
     framework predicts within 0.14 dex of Donato+09 with nothing fitted, and the 3.6 micron mass-to-light
     ratio it predicts from Lambda -- collapse by factors of 5 to 130, because both are read off a_0 and a_0
     is exactly what a census correction moves.
  4. IT DOES NOT TRANSFER.  The clusters' own number misses the rest of the positive ledger by a median
     0.46 dex, and no one- or two-parameter law gets the whole positive ledger below 0.26 dex of residual
     against a raw spread of 0.40.
  5. AND WITHIN THE CLUSTERS THE COMPONENT IS NOT BARYON-SHAPED.  It cannot be hot gas (1a: clumping is
     one-signed, and putting it in the emitting phase demands 4-39x the observed emission measure).  It is
     more centrally concentrated than the gas (1c), so it would have to pile up where the X-ray data are best.
     It does not follow the measured hot-gas content (3-hotgas: the trend has the wrong sign).  And the Bullet
     requires it to be collisionless and locked to the galaxies at 20-40x the stellar mass (1d) -- which is
     dark matter with a baryonic label, and section 5 shows what a component locked to stars at that ratio
     does to SPARC.

  THE ONE SURVIVING VERSION, stated plainly so that it is not mistaken for a success: a missing baryonic
  component that exists in hot haloes and NOT in galaxies closes the cluster front to 0.07 dex and breaks no
  keeper.  It is keeper-safe because it is switched off by hand in every system that constrains it.  That is
  one free amount per class of object, which is the freedom the framework exists to remove.

  ANGLE D IS A PARTIAL EXPLANATION OF THE CLUSTER FRONT AND NOT A UNIFICATION OF THE LEDGER.

